  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThis morning's rain has for the most part come to an end with \nseveral areas across central Kansas seeing 1-2.5\" of rainfall! \nAnother round is expected later tonight - more on this later. For \nthe remainder of the day today, broken cloud cover will limit much \nwarming as temperatures struggle to exceed the upper 70s. Looking at \nsynoptic features aloft, a large dome of high pressure still \ndominates the southern Plains with weak shortwave energy rounding \nthe northern axis of the ridge. By this evening and into the \novernight hours, the aforementioned shortwave will move into \nsouthern Nebraska and north-central Kansas increasing mid-level lift \nacross the area. A strengthening nocturnal LLJ will aid in low-level \nisentropic lift by midnight tonight as showers and thunderstorms \nspread across central KS. PWATs ranging from 2-2.25\" shows the \npossibility of very efficient rainfall rates overnight. The main \nquestion is where will the main axis of QPF fall through the \novernight hours. Guidance has stayed fairly consistent with the best \ndeep layer moisture staying along and north of I-70 and thus, \nhighest confidence in higher rainfall totals stays in this area \nthrough Friday morning. Areas south of I-70 will still see rain, but \ntotals may only range from 0.25\"-0.75\" with isolated pockets of 1 \ninch totals. Areas along and north of I-70 will have the best chance \nto see greater than 1 inch of rainfall with isolated areas seeing \ncloser to 2 inches. NBM probabilities of rain totals greater than an \ninch along and north of I-70 range from 50-80% with probs dropping \nto 25-50% for totals over 2 inches. Cannot rule out some isolated \nflooding occurring by Friday morning, especially in areas that saw \nheavy rainfall last night. Rain lingers across far eastern into the \nearly afternoon Friday that should help keep temperatures on the \ncool side yet again. Expectation is for areas across central Kansas \nto clear clouds by the late morning and increase temperatures into \nthe mid to upper 80s by the afternoon whereas areas further east may \nonly top out in the upper 70s and low 80s.\n\nDangerous heat will be the main headline for the weekend and into \nearly next week as the upper ridge begins to expand north across the \ncentral Plains during the day Saturday. Saturday will see highs \nincrease into the low to mid 90s before highs build into the upper \n90s and low 100s by Sunday and Monday. Confidence is fairly high for \nareas Sunday and Monday to see afternoon highs ranging from 95 to \n103 degrees given the small ensemble spreads. NBM probabilities for \nareas across central, north-central and along/south I-70 exceeding \n100 degrees Monday are currently sitting around 60-90%. Heat indices \neach day Saturday through next Tuesday will likely push into the 100-\n110 degree range.\n\nSome reprieve in the dangerous heat may come by Wednesday as the \nupper ridge sinks a bit south and allows mid-level energy/waves to \nfilter into eastern Kansas. This should allow temperatures to fall \nback closer to average with the increase in cloud cover and low-end \nprecipitation chances. Afternoon high temperature spreads by next \nweekend currently sit between the mid 80s and low 90s.",
